before:
```typescript
let seats: number = 6;
seats = 8;
let spare = 2;
console.log(seats + spare);
```

This prints 10. seats has an explicit number annotation. spare has no written annotation, but TypeScript infers number from its initial value. Both variables reject later text assignments. Inference is still checking, not permission to store any kind of value. console.log displays the sum; the type annotations themselves disappear from emitted JavaScript.

after:
Here, seats starts at 6 and changes to 8.
```typescript
let seats: number = 6;
seats = 8;
console.log(seats);
```
The output is 8. The type label helps check the code; it does not become part of the running JavaScript.
